window.X_SEARCH_INDEX = [{"title":"Home","url":"index.html","description":"","text":"Designers lead Designers set the direction, make the anchor screens and decide at both gates. Agents extend and check Agents do the work between the gates: they explore, extend from the anchors, check and hand over. People review what they produce. Buckholt never changes Agents build with Buckholt as it is. Anything Buckholt does not have is a flagged extension, registered for review."},{"title":"Agents","url":"agents/index.html","description":"","text":"Archive Keeps design context accurate and the same in the repo and SharePoint. Brief Turns a product feature into a UX problem and chooses the route. Insight Synthesises research and behaviour evidence. Map Turns findings into structure and a design worklist. Range Generates many quick, deliberately rough directions. Critic Stress tests the explorations before the shortlist gate. Extend Builds out the design from the designers' anchor screens. Align Checks consistency across all Figma work, designers' work included. Build Turns signed off designs into prototypes. Audit Runs automated quality checks before people see a prototype. Observe Supports usability testing around the sessions. Relay Hands over to engineering, then checks what was built."},{"title":"Archive","url":"agents/archive/index.html","description":"Keeps design context accurate and the same in the repo and SharePoint.","text":"Keeps design context accurate and the same in the repo and SharePoint. Cursor, plus a weekly scheduled run A design item reaching Discovery approved A merged change to /context The weekly check /context SharePoint discovery site Figma exports Azure DevOps design items On Discovery approved, write /context/discovery/ feature .md with the key findings, worklist and links to the full SharePoint documents. After a product pack change is merged, update its SharePoint copy. After each gate, add the decision to decision log.md from the gate notes. Weekly, flag packs older than 90 days, register entries missing their Figma or code half, and gates with no decision logged. Track daily Figma MCP calls and warn the team at 80% of the limit. Updated /context files SharePoint product pack copies Weekly health report Laurence or Jon approves every product pack change. Edit /buckholt or /legacy Change a pack without approval Delete history Repo and SharePoint copies match and the health report is posted."},{"title":"Brief","url":"agents/brief/index.html","description":"Turns a product feature into a UX problem and chooses the route.","text":"Turns a product feature into a UX problem and chooses the route. Microsoft 365 Copilot A feature set to Ready for design Azure DevOps feature and stories SharePoint specs Product pack copy Related Teams and Outlook threads Check the feature has a problem, outcome, products, users, priority, quarter and constraints. If not, comment the gaps on the feature and stop. Find the products and styling modes in the pack copies. Stop if a pack is missing. Choose the route. Fast track only if there is no new flow, no new research is needed and only existing screens change. Otherwise full. Record why. Write the UX brief from the template. Draft success measures and request baselines from the Insight LogRocket step. Save to SharePoint and link it on the design item. UX brief The PM confirms scope; a designer moves the status on. Rewrite the PM's feature or stories Invent baselines Start without a product pack The brief is linked and the PM has confirmed scope."},{"title":"Insight","url":"agents/insight/index.html","description":"Synthesises research and behaviour evidence.","text":"Synthesises research and behaviour evidence. Microsoft 365 Copilot, plus a Cursor step for LogRocket Status Discovery SharePoint research Teams transcripts Lucid boards UX brief LogRocket Cursor step List every source used. Anonymise names, emails, account numbers and any customer details. Group findings into themes, each with its evidence and product tag. Rank pain points by how often and how badly they occur. Cursor step: pull LogRocket baselines for the success measures completion, drop off, errors, rage clicks on the flows in scope. List evidence gaps and the research that would fill them. Save the discovery summary to SharePoint. Discovery summary with baselines Designers validate the insights. Present assumptions as findings Keep personal data Generalise from one user The summary is saved and every theme cites evidence."},{"title":"Map","url":"agents/map/index.html","description":"Turns findings into structure and a design worklist.","text":"Turns findings into structure and a design worklist. Microsoft 365 Copilot and Lucid The discovery summary being saved Discovery summary Product pack copy Existing Lucid flows Map the current journey in Lucid. Propose the future journey, or two options if the evidence splits. Write the screen inventory: new, changed and unchanged screens. Flag any clash with the product's navigation or patterns. Write the design worklist: screens, states and edge cases to design. Save to Lucid and SharePoint. Journey maps Screen inventory Design worklist Designers approve, then move the status to Discovery approved. Break a product's navigation pattern without flagging it Flows and worklist are approved."},{"title":"Range","url":"agents/range/index.html","description":"Generates many quick, deliberately rough directions.","text":"Generates many quick, deliberately rough directions. Cursor, sandbox only Discovery approved on a full route item /context/discovery/ feature .md Design worklist Patterns from other products Lucid flows Write 3 or 4 \"how might we\" prompts from the worklist. A designer may replace them. Generate 5 to 10 directions, including at least two that break the current pattern and opposing pairs guided vs expert, minimal vs dense . Build each as clickable greybox HTML in /sandbox/ product / feature / using greybox.css only. Add an \"Exploration, not for build\" banner to every page. Write an index page: each direction, its one line idea and trade offs. Post the index link on the design item. Sandbox explorations Index page Use Buckholt, brand colours or tokens Write outside the sandbox Polish The index is posted."},{"title":"Critic","url":"agents/critic/index.html","description":"Stress-tests the explorations before the shortlist gate.","text":"Stress tests the explorations before the shortlist gate. Cursor The Range index being posted Sandbox explorations /context/discovery/ feature .md Product rules For each direction, assess user needs: which themes it serves and which it misses. Check edge cases: empty, error, loading, permissions, heavy data, long text. Check consistency with the product's patterns and styling mode. Estimate build effort low, medium or high with the reason. List risks. Recommend up to three directions, labelled as a recommendation. critique.md in the sandbox feature folder, linked on the design item The shortlist gate designers, PM, engineer decides. Make the decision Judge taste or brand Every direction has a critique."},{"title":"Extend","url":"agents/extend/index.html","description":"Builds out the design from the designers' anchor screens.","text":"Builds out the design from the designers' anchor screens. Cursor + Figma MCP the only agent with write access Status Anchors ready Frames labelled Anchor The product's Figma library Design worklist Figma usage log Read the anchors and list the layout rules, components, spacing and text styles used. Work through the worklist in batches of up to 5 screens. Use library components as instances; never detach them. Add every state and edge case on the worklist. If a component is missing, build it on the Extensions page with an x/ prefix and add a register entry. After each batch, leave a note: what was built and what was assumed. Apply feedback changes when asked. Remaining screens States and variants Flagged components Designers review every batch. Detach or change library components Edit anchors Work without anchors The worklist is covered and Align has run."},{"title":"Align","url":"agents/align/index.html","description":"Checks consistency across all Figma work, designers' work included.","text":"Checks consistency across all Figma work, designers' work included. Cursor + Figma MCP read only Each Extend batch A designer's request Weekly on active files The named Figma pages only The product library The styling mode The extensions register Check for detached instances and styles not from the library. Check spacing and sizes against the token scale. Check for wrong or one off text styles. Check wording against the product pack's terms. Check for styling mode mismatches, such as Buckholt parts in a legacy screen. Check extension components are flagged and register entries have both halves. Issue list by severity with frame links, posted on the design item Designers decide what is a mistake and what is deliberate. Fix anything Change files The report is posted."},{"title":"Build","url":"agents/build/index.html","description":"Turns signed-off designs into prototypes.","text":"Turns signed off designs into prototypes. Cursor Status Design signed off Figma frames read only The product pack /buckholt or /legacy /extensions The prototype build spec Create /prototypes/ product / feature /. Buckholt mode: use Buckholt HTML and CSS. Legacy mode: use the legacy CSS. Hybrid: follow the pack, with every Buckholt element labelled. For a missing component, reuse a register entry if one exists; otherwise build it in /extensions/ product / with tokens only, an x prefix, data buckholt=\"false\" and a register entry. Wire the interactions the test tasks need. Publish the preview and post the link on the design item. Prototype Extensions Preview link Designers check fidelity and behaviour. Write to /buckholt or /legacy Import from /sandbox Hard code values Edit Figma The preview is posted and Audit has passed."},{"title":"Audit","url":"agents/audit/index.html","description":"Runs automated quality checks before people see a prototype.","text":"Runs automated quality checks before people see a prototype. Cursor + Playwright + axe Any new or changed prototype The prototype /tests The Buckholt HTML/CSS reference or legacy CSS Run axe on every page. Check the keyboard path through each test task, focus order and visible focus. Check contrast and labels. Check markup and classes against the Buckholt reference or legacy CSS . Flag hard coded colours, spacing and fonts. Flag x components missing a register entry. Audit report by severity, posted on the design item Blocks testing when a critical accessibility issue is found. Fix code Check the sandbox No critical issues remain."},{"title":"Observe","url":"agents/observe/index.html","description":"Supports usability testing around the sessions.","text":"Supports usability testing around the sessions. Cursor Audit passing and sessions being booked The prototype Success measures Teams recordings LogRocket baselines Draft the test plan and script from the success measures and test tasks. After sessions, tag recordings by task, success, hesitation, error and quote. Summarise per task and compare with the LogRocket baselines. Write findings: issue, how many participants, severity, suggested change, and whether it goes back to Extend or Build. Test plan Tagged clips Findings report Designers run the sessions and decide what changes. Run sessions Treat one participant's comment as a finding Keep personal data Findings are posted and routed."},{"title":"Relay","url":"agents/relay/index.html","description":"Hands over to engineering, then checks what was built.","text":"Hands over to engineering, then checks what was built. Cursor + Azure DevOps MCP A designer marking testing complete Final Figma screens The prototype Buckholt Storybook The extensions register Write a spec per screen: states, edge cases, interactions and content. Map each element to a Storybook component or a flagged extension. Write UX acceptance criteria Given, When, Then for each engineering story. Add them to the stories as a linked UX section with Figma and prototype links, and set the predecessor link. Update Design status on the feature to Handed over. After release, compare the build with the design, and LogRocket data with the success measures. Post the report. Specs UX acceptance criteria Linked stories Release check Designers review before handoff; engineering reviews feasibility. Hand over anything not signed off Edit the PM's story text Change engineering statuses Every story has its UX section and the release check is posted."},{"title":"Workflow","url":"workflow/index.html","description":"Work moves through six stages and two gates. Agents do the work in each stage; designers, the PM and an engineer decide at the gates.","text":"The workflow at a glance Six designers, one workflow The UX team is six designers. Everyone works through the same workflow, on the same board, with the same agents. The proposal for sharing the work: Every design item has one lead designer , set in Assigned to on the design board. The lead moves the item through its statuses, makes or agrees the anchor screens, and brings the item to each gate. A second designer reviews. Each design item also names a reviewing designer, who reviews agent output alongside the lead, such as batches from Extend and reports from Align. \"Designers\" at a gate means at least two of the six : the lead and the reviewing designer, with the PM and an engineer. The agents are shared. The Cursor agents come from the repo, so all six designers run exactly the same rules; changes go through a pull request. The Copilot agents are created once and shared with the team. Two owners, a delegate and contributors. Laurence and Jon own the workflow and the agent definitions. Marijana is their delegate and acts for them. Katerina, Andrej and Mark are contributors: anyone can propose a change by pull request. Stages Each stage has its own agents. An agent starts when the design item reaches the status that triggers it, and stops at a human check. Stage Agents Where they run Foundation Archive Cursor, plus a weekly scheduled run Discover Brief Insight Map Microsoft 365 Copilot, with Lucid and a Cursor step for LogRocket Explore Range Critic Cursor, in the sandbox Design Extend Align Cursor and Figma MCP Prototype Build Audit Observe Cursor, with Playwright and axe Handoff Relay Cursor and the Azure DevOps MCP Between Explore and Design, designers make the anchor screens in Figma. Extend builds out from them and never works without them. Gates There are two gates. At each one, designers, the PM and an engineer decide together, using the evidence the agents have produced. Stage statuses The design item moves through these statuses in order. Some of them start an agent. Status What it starts Discovery Insight synthesises research and behaviour evidence. Discovery approved Archive writes the discovery summary into the repo. Range starts on full route items. Shortlist agreed Set when the shortlist gate passes. Designers make the anchor screens. Anchors ready Extend builds out the design from the anchors. Design signed off Set when the design sign off gate passes. Build starts the prototype. Handed over Set on the feature by Relay. Engineering stories are ready. Brief starts earlier, when product sets the feature to Ready for design. Map's output is approved by designers, who then move the status to Discovery approved. Fast track Brief chooses the route for every feature and records why. The fast track goes from Discover straight to Design, skipping Explore and the shortlist gate. Use the fast track only when all three are true: there is no new flow no new research is needed only existing screens change Otherwise the feature takes the full route. Test loop Prototypes are tested with people. Observe writes up the findings, and each finding says whether it goes back to Extend a design change or Build a prototype change . Designers decide what changes. The loop repeats until testing is complete; then Relay hands over. Cadence under scaled agile Design runs one sprint ahead of engineering: engineering stories are ready only when the design is Handed over. The proposed rhythm: When What happens Before PI planning Product sets the features expected in the next planning increment to Ready for design. Brief writes their UX briefs and chooses each route. PI planning UX commits its sprint capacity against those features, using the routes to size them: fast track items need less than full route items. Each sprint Design items move through their statuses. A feature planned for engineering in a sprint should reach Handed over by the end of the sprint before it. Each week Gates are held at a fixed time, so a design item never waits more than a week for a decision. Archive runs its weekly check. After release Relay checks the build against the design and the success measures. Findings feed the next planning increment."},{"title":"Toolset","url":"toolset/index.html","description":"Discovery happens in Microsoft 365. Exploring, designing and prototyping happen in Cursor. Archive keeps the two in step.","text":"Two platforms and a bridge Microsoft 365 side Discovery happens here: Brief, Insight and Map run in Copilot. Cursor side Exploring, designing and prototyping happen here: the other nine agents run in Cursor. The bridge Archive keeps the two sides in step. It copies product packs into SharePoint after a change is merged, and pulls approved discovery into the repo as /context/discovery/ feature .md . Access needed Foundations come first, then connectors and access, then the agents. Access Who needs it Figma MCP, write Extend. Needs a Full seat. Figma read and Code Connect Align, Build, Relay Legacy Figma libraries Anchor screens, Extend, Align LogRocket MCP Insight, Observe, Relay Lucid MCP Map, Range Azure DevOps connector Brief, to read features Archive tracks daily Figma MCP calls and warns the team at 80% of the limit. The set up guides /setup guides/ take you through each one in order."},{"title":"Repo and Buckholt","url":"repo-and-buckholt/index.html","description":"Buckholt never changes. Agents create only in three folders, and everything they make outside Buckholt is flagged and registered.","text":"Repo zones Proposed layout: These folder names are the proposed layout for the prototyping repo. They are not all in place yet. Locks /buckholt holds Buckholt core and /legacy/ product holds captured legacy CSS. No agent writes to either. Changing them needs the owner's approval. Every agent that touches code is told so in its rules: Archive never edits them, Build never writes to them. Extensions When Buckholt has no component for what a design needs, it becomes an extension. In code, Build first reuses an existing register entry. If there is none, it builds the component in /extensions/ product / . In Figma, Extend builds it on the Extensions page with an x/ prefix. Every code extension follows the same rules: Buckholt tokens only CSS custom properties . No hard coded colours, spacing, radii or fonts. Class prefix x and the attribute data buckholt=\"false\" . One entry per component in the extensions register, with both halves: Figma and code. Align checks that extension components are flagged and register entries have both halves. Audit flags x components with no register entry. Archive flags register entries missing a half in its weekly check. html \"An extension, flagged\" ... Sandbox Range works only in /sandbox/ product / feature / . Explorations are clickable greybox HTML using greybox.css only, with an \"Exploration, not for build\" banner on every page. They never use Buckholt, brand colours or tokens. Nothing in the sandbox is ever promoted. Build never imports from it, and Audit does not check it. Styling modes Each product pack records the product's styling mode. Build follows it: Mode What Build uses Buckholt Buckholt HTML and CSS Legacy The product's legacy CSS Hybrid What the pack says, with every Buckholt element labelled Align checks for styling mode mismatches, such as Buckholt parts in a legacy screen. Rules files /agents holds one file per agent: its job, triggers, steps, limits and when it is done. Laurence and Jon write them. /.cursor/rules holds the rules every agent follows. Both change only by pull request. Each agent page on this site shows its rules file. Tests /tests holds the Playwright and axe checks. Audit runs them on every new or changed prototype."},{"title":"Artifacts and templates","url":"artifacts-and-templates/index.html","description":"Seven artifacts carry the work between agents. Each has one writer, one home, and named readers.","text":"The seven artifacts Artifact Written by Where it lives Read by Product pack Archive, with Laurence or Jon approving Repo /context , copy in SharePoint All agents UX brief Brief SharePoint, linked on the design item Insight, Map, the PM Discovery summary Insight and Map SharePoint, summary copy in the repo Range, Critic, Build, Observe Decision log Archive, from gate outcomes Repo /context All agents, Jim, PMs Extensions register Extend and Build Repo /context Align, Audit, Relay Migration map Archive Repo /context Build, Align Agent definition Laurence and Jon Repo /agents The agent itself Where each one comes from Product pack : one per product, including its styling mode. A change is merged in the repo, then Archive updates the SharePoint copy. Laurence or Jon approves every change. UX brief : Brief writes it from the template once the feature has everything it needs, and links it on the design item. Discovery summary : Insight lists every source, anonymises personal details and groups findings into themes with evidence. On Discovery approved, Archive writes the repo copy to /context/discovery/ feature .md . Decision log : after each gate, Archive adds the decision to decision log.md from the gate notes. Extensions register : one entry per extension, with a Figma half and a code half. Agent definition : one file per agent in /agents , changed by pull request. Templates The agents work from shared templates. Each one below follows what the agent's rules say it must contain. Copy a template to use it. UX brief Written by Brief, saved to SharePoint and linked on the design item. markdown \"UX brief template\" UX brief: feature name Design item: link Feature: link Date: date Problem The user problem, in one or two sentences. Outcome What changes for users and the business when this is done. Products and styling mode Product : Buckholt, Hybrid or Legacy Users Who this is for. Priority and quarter Priority · Quarter Constraints Constraint Route Full or Fast track Why: No new flow? No new research needed? Only existing screens change? Success measures Measure Baseline from Insight Target Completion to request target Drop off to request target Errors to request target Scope confirmed by the PM Name, date Discovery summary Written by Insight, saved to SharePoint, then copied into the repo by Archive. markdown \"Discovery summary template\" Discovery summary: feature name Sources SRC 01 Source, link, date Themes Theme Evidence: SRC 01, SRC 02 Products: product What we found. Pain points, ranked Pain point How often How badly Evidence Baselines LogRocket Flow Completion Drop off Errors Rage clicks Evidence gaps Gap : research that would fill it All personal data removed: yes Critique Written by Critic as critique.md in the sandbox feature folder. markdown \"Critique template\" Critique: feature name Direction n : name User needs served: themes User needs missed: themes Edge cases: empty error loading permissions heavy data long text Consistency with product patterns and styling mode: notes Build effort: low, medium or high , because reason Risks: risks Recommendation for the shortlist gate to decide 1. Direction , because reason Decision log entry Added to decision log.md by Archive after each gate. markdown \"Decision log entry template\" Date · Feature · Shortlist gate or Design sign off gate People: designers, PM, engineer Decision: what was decided Evidence: links Status set to: Shortlist agreed or Design signed off Extensions register entry Added by Extend Figma half and Build code half . markdown \"Extensions register entry template\" ID Component Product Why Buckholt does not cover it Figma x/ name, link Code x class, path Tokens used Status X n name product reason x/name x name, /extensions/product/ tokens draft, reviewed, approved Align and Audit reports Posted on the design item, issues listed by severity. markdown \"Report template\" Align or Audit report: feature name , date Severity Issue Where frame or page link Rule Critical issue link rule Serious issue link rule Minor issue link rule Test findings Written by Observe after the sessions. markdown \"Test findings template\" Test findings: feature name Task Success Compared with baseline Findings Issue Participants Severity Suggested change Goes back to issue n of n severity change Extend or Build UX section on a story Added to each engineering story by Relay. markdown \"Story UX section template\" UX Figma: link Prototype: link Spec: link UX acceptance criteria Given context , when action , then result . States Empty · Loading · Error · Success · Permissions Components Element : Storybook component or flagged extension Agent definition One file per agent in /agents , written by Laurence and Jon and changed by pull request. Each agent page on this site shows its file in this shape. markdown \"Agent definition template\" Agent name Stage: stage Tool: tool Job: one sentence Triggered by Reads Steps Produces Human gate Never Done when The sandbox uses one more shared file: greybox.css , the only styling Range may use, so explorations stay deliberately rough."},{"title":"Azure DevOps","url":"azure-devops/index.html","description":"Design has its own board in Azure DevOps. Each design item links into the product feature above it and the engineering stories after it.","text":"How the work items link The design board The UX design board belongs to the UX team. Its stage statuses trigger the agents, so the board is how the workflow runs, not just a record of it. One board for the whole team All six designers share one UX design board. One board means one view of everything in design, one Delivery Plan, and one place the agents watch for status changes. Separate boards would split all three. The proposal for making one board work for six people: Setting How it is used Assigned to The lead designer for the design item. Filter the board by person to see one designer's work. Reviewing designer A field on the design item naming the second designer. Area path One per product, so the board can be filtered by product, and product teams see their own design items. Swimlanes By route: Full route, Fast track and Blocked below . Work in progress limits On Designing and on Prototyping and testing, so no one carries too many items at once. At stand up, walk the board right to left: items closest to Handed over first. The design item There is one design item per feature. UX owns it and its columns. Its parent is the product feature. It is the predecessor of the engineering stories. It links to the SharePoint UX brief, the Figma file and the prototype. Agents post their outputs on it: the Range index, the Critic review, Align and Audit reports, and the prototype preview. Fields Before design starts, Brief checks the feature has these fields complete. If any are missing, it comments the gaps on the feature and stops. Problem Outcome Products Users Priority Quarter Constraints The feature also carries a Design status field. The design item's status rolls up to it, and Relay sets it to Handed over. Columns The design item moves through the stage statuses: See the workflow /workflow/ for what each status starts. Links to product and engineering The portfolio epic is the parent of the feature. Both belong to product. The feature is planned per quarter and triggers design when it is set to Ready for design. Engineering stories are ready only when design is Handed over. Relay adds a linked UX section to each story, with UX acceptance criteria, Figma and prototype links, and sets the predecessor link. Relay never changes engineering statuses or the PM's story text. Swimlanes The proposed swimlanes on the design board: Swimlane What goes in it Full route Features that go through Explore and both gates. Fast track Features Brief has fast tracked: from Discover straight to Design. Blocked Items waiting on something outside UX, such as missing feature fields, a missing product pack or access. The item keeps its status; the swimlane shows why it is not moving. Delivery Plans One Delivery Plan shows the three boards together by sprint: product features, from the product board design items, from the UX design board engineering stories, from the engineering boards Each design item's target date is the end of the sprint before its engineering stories start. That makes the one sprint offset visible, and shows straight away when design is at risk of holding up engineering."},{"title":"Working with product","url":"working-with-product/index.html","description":"Product and UX meet through a contract, not through each other's agents.","text":"The contract Two things cross between them: Ready for design , from product to UX, and UX outputs on stories , from UX to product. What we consume A feature set to Ready for design . The status is the trigger. The feature fields: problem, outcome, products, users, priority, quarter and constraints. Brief will not start until they are complete. The feature's stories, SharePoint specs and related Teams and Outlook threads, which Brief reads. The PM's confirmation of scope on the UX brief. What we publish The UX brief and draft success measures, linked on the design item. The Design status field on the feature, which shows where design is. At handoff, a linked UX section on each engineering story: UX acceptance criteria Given, When, Then , Figma and prototype links, and the predecessor link. After release, a check by Relay of the build against the design, and of LogRocket data against the success measures. Working alongside other agents Product has its own agents, including story writing agents. Ours never do their work or change theirs: Brief never rewrites the PM's feature or stories. Relay never edits the PM's story text and never changes engineering statuses. Relay never hands over anything that has not been signed off. If a feature is missing fields, Brief comments the gaps on the feature and stops. It does not fill them in. Where product joins in The PM confirms scope on the UX brief. The PM sits on both gates, with designers and an engineer. PMs read the UX brief and the decision log."},{"title":"Set-up guides","url":"setup-guides/index.html","description":"Set up the foundations first, then connectors and access, then the agents. Each guide builds on the one before.","text":"The order 1. Foundations : the repo and its locks, product packs, the design board and the SharePoint site. 2. Connectors and access : Figma MCP write for Extend, read and Code Connect for the others , LogRocket MCP, Lucid MCP, the Azure DevOps connector and the legacy Figma libraries. 3. Agents : create the nine Cursor agents and the three Microsoft 365 Copilot agents guides 10 and 11 . The guides Adding a guide Each guide is a Markdown file in agentic/content/guides/setup guides/ , named with its position, for example 03 figma mcp.md . The site numbers them, shows progress and links each one to the next. markdown \"Guide file template\" title: Connect Figma MCP lead: One sentence on why this matters. Steps 1. First step. 2. Second step. Docs Link text https://example.com/docs Watch Video title https://example.com/video"},{"title":"How-to guides","url":"how-to-guides/index.html","description":"Short guides for the jobs that come up again and again.","text":"Guides"},{"title":"Governance and rollout","url":"governance/index.html","description":"People own every decision. This page says who owns what, how work is reviewed and how we will know the workflow is working.","text":"Ownership What Owner The workflow and this site Owners: Laurence Abbott and Jon. Delegate: Marijana The design team Laurence and Jon owners , Marijana delegate , Katerina, Andrej and Mark contributors Agent definitions in /agents Laurence and Jon, changed by pull request Rules in /.cursor/rules Changed by pull request Product packs Laurence or Jon approves every change Buckholt core and legacy CSS Their owners; no agent writes to them Each design item Its lead designer, set in Assigned to, with a reviewing designer Direction and scope The shortlist gate: designers, PM, engineer Design sign off The sign off gate: designers, PM, engineer Reviews Every agent stops at a human check. The main ones: Designers validate Insight's insights and approve Map's flows and worklist. Designers review every Extend batch. Designers decide which of Align's issues are mistakes and which are deliberate. Designers check Build's prototypes for fidelity and behaviour. Audit blocks testing when it finds a critical accessibility issue. Designers review before Relay hands over; engineering reviews feasibility. Each agent's page lists its human gate. Keeping the system healthy Archive runs a weekly check and posts a health report. It flags: product packs older than 90 days extension register entries missing their Figma or code half gates with no decision logged It also tracks daily Figma MCP calls and warns the team at 80% of the limit. After each gate, Archive adds the decision to the decision log. Measures Every feature has success measures. 1. Brief drafts them in the UX brief. 2. Insight pulls LogRocket baselines for them: completion, drop off, errors and rage clicks on the flows in scope. 3. Observe compares test results with the baselines. 4. After release, Relay compares LogRocket data with the success measures and posts the report. For the workflow itself, the proposed measures are: Measure What it tells us Source Time from Ready for design to Handed over Whether design keeps one sprint ahead Azure DevOps Time in each status Where work waits Azure DevOps Gates passed first time Whether evidence is ready when the gate meets Decision log Findings sent back by the test loop How much rework testing finds Observe findings Critical issues found by Audit Quality before people see a prototype Audit reports Extensions reused against created Whether the register is working Extensions register Rollout phases The proposed rollout adds the agents in workflow order, one phase at a time, with a pilot feature first. Phase What is set up Ready to move on when 1. Foundations Repo and locks, product packs, the design board, the SharePoint site. Archive starts. A pack exists for the pilot product and the board runs on stage statuses. 2. Discover Connectors for Copilot, Azure DevOps and Lucid. Brief, Insight and Map. One pilot feature reaches Discovery approved. 3. Explore and Design The sandbox, Figma MCP and Lucid MCP. Range, Critic, Extend and Align. The pilot passes the design sign off gate. 4. Prototype and handoff /tests , LogRocket MCP and the Azure DevOps MCP. Build, Audit, Observe and Relay. The pilot is Handed over and the release check is posted. 5. All features Every new feature goes through the workflow. Reviewed after one planning increment. Open questions Which product and feature should be the pilot? How many Figma Full seats are needed for Extend's write access? Where should the extensions register live: one in /context , or one per product? Who approves changes to /.cursor/rules ? Do fast tracked changes need new anchor screens, or do the current screens act as anchors? The /agents rules files do not exist yet. Until they do, each agent page shows rules generated from agents.json ."},{"title":"Team capacity","url":"team-capacity/index.html","description":"How many designers the workflow needs each quarter, and how much the agents save. The model is a live spreadsheet that the team edits together.","text":"The live model The model is a shared spreadsheet. Anyone it is shared with can open it; editors can change it, and their changes save straight away. There is one copy, so whoever opens it next, or refreshes it, sees the latest figures. Placeholder figures: The numbers in the model are placeholders. Replace the input cells with real figures after the pilot. What you change Inputs: products supported features per quarter, across all products the share of features on the full workflow the rest take the fast track weeks per quarter working hours per week, per designer the share of time usable for feature work weeks off per designer per quarter designers in the team Human hours per feature, by step , for the full workflow, the fast track, and the full workflow without agents. The steps follow the workflow, from reviewing the brief with the PM to reviewing the handoff from Relay. Fixed work each quarter : running the agents and Archive, product packs, onboarding legacy products, the design system and extensions register, planning and sprint ceremonies, and support. What it works out full workflow and fast track features per quarter human hours per feature, and in total per quarter usable hours per designer per quarter designers needed , exactly and rounded up designers needed without agents , to show what the agents save how many features per quarter the team could handle the biggest human step on the full workflow Using it Change only the input and hours cells. The results are formulas. Use the step hours to see where designer time goes, and which agent would save the most if it improved. Compare \"designers needed\" with the designers in the team, and the features the team could handle with the features planned. Version history in the spreadsheet shows who changed what, and can restore an earlier version."},{"title":"Repo and locks","url":"setup-guides/repo-and-locks/index.html","description":"Agents can only be trusted with the repo if the folders they must never change are locked.","text":"Who does it Laurence or Jon, with a GitHub organisation admin. What you need Admin access to the Buckholt prototyping repo Buckholt core and any captured legacy CSS, ready to move into their folders Steps 1. Create the folders: /buckholt , /legacy , /extensions , /sandbox , /prototypes , /context , /agents , /.cursor/rules and /tests . The repo zones /repo and buckholt/ explain what each one is for. 2. Move Buckholt core into /buckholt and any captured legacy CSS into /legacy/ product . 3. Add a CODEOWNERS file so changes to the locked folders need their owners' review. 4. Turn on branch protection for main : require pull requests and code owner review. 5. Add a check that fails any pull request that changes /buckholt or /legacy without owner approval. 6. Add the shared rules to /.cursor/rules , and one rules file per agent to /agents . text \"CODEOWNERS\" /buckholt/ Buckholt owners team /legacy/ Buckholt owners team /agents/ workflow owners /.cursor/ workflow owners Done when A pull request that touches /buckholt cannot be merged without an owner's approval, and every agent has its rules file. Used by Every agent. Build, Extend and Range rely on the locks most. Docs external sites About code owners, GitHub Docs https://docs.github.com/en/repositories/managing your repositorys settings and features/customizing your repository/about code owners About protected branches, GitHub Docs https://docs.github.com/en/repositories/configuring branches and merges in your repository/managing protected branches/about protected branches"},{"title":"Product packs","url":"setup-guides/product-packs/index.html","description":"Every agent reads the product pack, so each product needs one before any feature for it can start.","text":"Who does it A designer who knows the product, with Laurence or Jon approving. What you need The product's styling mode: Buckholt, Hybrid or Legacy Its navigation pattern, key screens and terms The repo set up, with /context in place Steps 1. Create the pack in /context , one file per product, from the outline below. 2. Record the styling mode. Build follows it and Align checks against it. 3. List the product's terms, so Align can check wording. 4. Open a pull request. Laurence or Jon approves it. 5. Once it is merged, Archive copies the pack to SharePoint. markdown \"Product pack outline\" Product pack: product Styling mode: Buckholt, Hybrid or Legacy Owner: name Last reviewed: date Users Navigation pattern Key screens Terms Use Not Figma library Legacy CSS Legacy and Hybrid only Done when Each product in scope has an approved pack in /context and a matching copy in SharePoint. Used by All agents. Brief stops if a pack is missing."},{"title":"Design board","url":"setup-guides/design-board/index.html","description":"The design board runs the workflow. Its stage statuses are what start each agent.","text":"Who does it Laurence or Jon, with an Azure DevOps project administrator. What you need Rights to edit the process and create a board in the Azure DevOps project Agreement with product on the Design status field on features Steps 1. Create the UX design board for the UX team. 2. Add a design item work item type, with one design item per feature. 3. Set the board's columns to the ten stage statuses, in order: Intake, Discovery, Discovery approved, Exploring, Shortlist agreed, Anchors ready, Designing, Design signed off, Prototyping and testing, Handed over. 4. Add the swimlanes: Full route, Fast track and Blocked. 5. Add all six designers to the UX team, add a Reviewing designer field to the design item, and create one area path per product. 6. Set work in progress limits on Designing and on Prototyping and testing. 7. Add a Design status field to features, and roll the design item's status up to it. 8. Set the links: the feature is the parent of the design item; the design item is the predecessor of the engineering stories. 9. Add the hyperlinks on the design item: SharePoint brief, Figma file, prototype. 10. Create a Delivery Plan with the product, design and engineering boards. Done when A test feature set to Ready for design gets a design item, moves across the columns, and shows its Design status on the feature. Used by Every agent is started by a status on this board. Relay sets Handed over. Docs external sites Azure Boards documentation, Microsoft Learn https://learn.microsoft.com/en us/azure/devops/boards/"},{"title":"SharePoint site","url":"setup-guides/sharepoint-site/index.html","description":"SharePoint holds the discovery master copy and a copy of every product pack, so people outside Cursor can read them.","text":"Who does it Laurence or Jon, with a SharePoint site owner. What you need A SharePoint site for the UX team, or rights to create one Steps 1. Create the site, or a section of the UX team's site. 2. Add three libraries: UX briefs, Discovery, and Product packs. 3. Give the UX team edit access and product managers read access. 4. Give Copilot access, so Brief, Insight and Map can read and save there. 5. Give Archive access, so it can copy packs in and pull approved discovery out. Done when A UX brief saved in SharePoint can be linked on a design item, and Archive can copy a pack into the Product packs library. Used by Brief, Insight, Map and Archive. Docs external sites SharePoint in Microsoft 365, Microsoft Learn https://learn.microsoft.com/en us/sharepoint/"},{"title":"Figma MCP","url":"setup-guides/figma-mcp/index.html","description":"Figma MCP lets agents read the designs, and lets one agent, Extend, build them out.","text":"Who does it Each designer, on their own machine, once their Figma seat is in place. What you need Cursor installed A Figma Full seat for anyone running Extend, because it writes Read access to the product's Figma files and libraries for everyone else Steps 1. Add the Figma MCP server to Cursor. 2. Sign in to Figma when Cursor asks. 3. Check read access: ask Cursor to list the frames on a page you can see. 4. For Extend only: check write access on a test file. 5. Set up Code Connect, so Align, Build and Relay can map Figma components to code. Done when Cursor can read a named Figma page, and the people running Extend can write to a test file. Used by Extend writes. Align, Build and Relay only read. Archive tracks daily calls and warns at 80% of the limit. Docs external sites Model Context Protocol MCP , Cursor Docs https://cursor.com/docs/mcp Figma Help Center https://help.figma.com/"},{"title":"Legacy Figma libraries","url":"setup-guides/legacy-figma-libraries/index.html","description":"Products not yet on Buckholt need their own Figma library, so anchors and extended screens match how the product looks today.","text":"Who does it A designer for each legacy product. What you need The product's existing Figma components The product pack, with its styling mode set to Legacy or Hybrid Steps 1. Publish the product's components as a Figma library. 2. Name it after the product, so Extend and Align pick the right one. 3. Record the library in the product pack. 4. Enable it in the product's design files. Done when Designers can make anchor screens from the library, and Align reports no \"not from the library\" issues on a test frame. Used by Designers making anchor screens, Extend and Align. Docs external sites Figma Help Center https://help.figma.com/"},{"title":"LogRocket MCP","url":"setup-guides/logrocket-mcp/index.html","description":"LogRocket gives every feature real baselines, so success measures are based on what users actually do.","text":"Who does it Each person who runs Insight's Cursor step, Observe or Relay. What you need Cursor installed A LogRocket account with access to the products in scope Steps 1. Add the LogRocket MCP server to Cursor. 2. Sign in to LogRocket when Cursor asks. 3. Check access: ask for the completion rate of a known flow. 4. Agree which flows each product's success measures use. Done when Insight can pull completion, drop off, error and rage click baselines for a flow in scope. Used by Insight Cursor step , Observe and Relay. Docs external sites Model Context Protocol MCP , Cursor Docs https://cursor.com/docs/mcp LogRocket documentation https://docs.logrocket.com/"},{"title":"Lucid","url":"setup-guides/lucid-mcp/index.html","description":"Lucid holds the journey maps from Discover and the rough flows from Explore.","text":"Who does it Each designer. What you need A Lucid account in the OGI workspace Cursor installed, for Range Steps 1. Connect Lucid to Microsoft 365 Copilot, so Map can create and update journey maps. 2. Add the Lucid MCP server to Cursor, so Range can draw rough flows. 3. Create a Lucid folder per product for journeys and flows. 4. Check access: ask Map to open an existing flow. Done when Map can save a journey map and Range can read it. Used by Map and Range. Docs external sites Model Context Protocol MCP , Cursor Docs https://cursor.com/docs/mcp Lucid Help Center https://help.lucid.co/"},{"title":"Azure DevOps connectors","url":"setup-guides/azure-devops-connectors/index.html","description":"Agents read features and write to stories through Azure DevOps, so the work and the record stay in one place.","text":"Who does it Laurence or Jon, with an Azure DevOps project administrator, then each person who runs Relay. What you need The design board set up Microsoft 365 Copilot, for Brief Cursor, for Relay Steps 1. Connect Azure DevOps to Microsoft 365 Copilot, so Brief can read features, stories and their fields. 2. Add the Azure DevOps MCP server to Cursor, so Relay can add UX sections and links to stories. 3. Limit Relay's access: it may add to stories and set Design status, but never change engineering statuses or the PM's story text. 4. Check both: Brief reads a test feature; Relay adds a UX section to a test story. Done when A test feature runs from Ready for design to Handed over, with Brief reading it and Relay writing the UX section. Used by Brief and Relay. Docs external sites Model Context Protocol MCP , Cursor Docs https://cursor.com/docs/mcp Azure DevOps documentation, Microsoft Learn https://learn.microsoft.com/en us/azure/devops/"},{"title":"Create agents in Cursor","url":"setup-guides/create-agents-in-cursor/index.html","description":"Nine of the twelve agents run in Cursor. Each one is a rules file that points Cursor's Agent at that agent's definition.","text":"Who does it Laurence and Jon write and approve the agent definitions; Marijana can approve as their delegate. Each of the six designers sets up Cursor on their own machine; because the rules live in the repo, everyone runs the same agents. What you need Cursor installed, signed in to the OGI team account The repo set up, with /agents and /.cursor/rules in place guide 1 /setup guides/repo and locks/ The connectors the agent uses guides 5 to 9 How it fits together The agent definition lives in /agents/ agent .md . It is the source of truth: job, triggers, what it reads, steps, never, done when. Each agent page on this site shows it. A project rule in /.cursor/rules/ agent .mdc tells Cursor's Agent when to use that definition. Project rules are .mdc files, kept in git with the code. A shared rule in /.cursor/rules applies to every agent, every time: the locks, Buckholt first, and the extension rules. MCP servers in /.cursor/mcp.json give the agents their tools: Figma, LogRocket, Lucid and Azure DevOps. The nine Cursor agents are Archive, Range, Critic, Extend, Align, Build, Audit, Observe and Relay. Insight also has a Cursor step for LogRocket. Steps 1. Write the agent definition in /agents/ agent .md from the agent definition template /artifacts and templates/ , and open a pull request. Laurence or Jon approves it. 2. Add the shared rule once, in /.cursor/rules/workflow.mdc , set to always apply. 3. Add a project rule for the agent in /.cursor/rules/ agent .mdc . Give it a clear description, so Agent knows when it is relevant, and point it at the definition file. You can type /create rule in Agent and describe the rule to have Cursor draft it. 4. Scope it where that helps. Range's rule, for example, applies to files in /sandbox . 5. Add the MCP servers the agent needs to /.cursor/mcp.json , using each provider's own set up instructions. 6. Try it on a test design item: open Agent, name the agent and the design item, and check it follows its steps and stops at its human gate. 7. Change it only by pull request , so every change to an agent is reviewed and recorded. markdown \"Shared rule: /.cursor/rules/workflow.mdc\" description: Rules every UX agent follows alwaysApply: true Never edit /buckholt or /legacy. Check Buckholt core and its documentation before making anything new. Anything Buckholt does not have goes in /extensions/ product /: Buckholt tokens only, class prefix x , data buckholt=\"false\", and an entry in the extensions register. Stop at your human gate. Post what you produced on the design item. markdown \"Agent rule: /.cursor/rules/range.mdc\" description: Range agent. Use when a full route design item reaches Discovery approved and needs rough directions explored. globs: sandbox/ alwaysApply: false You are the Range agent. Follow @agents/range.md exactly. Work only in /sandbox/ product / feature / and use greybox.css only. json \"MCP servers: /.cursor/mcp.json\" { \"mcpServers\": { \"figma\": { \"url\": \" server address from Figma's set up instructions \" }, \"logrocket\": { \"url\": \" server address from LogRocket's set up instructions \" }, \"lucid\": { \"url\": \" server address from Lucid's set up instructions \" }, \"azure devops\": { \"command\": \" command from the Azure DevOps MCP set up instructions \" } } } Done when Each Cursor agent has a definition in /agents , a project rule in /.cursor/rules , and has run once on a test design item, stopping at its human gate. Docs external sites Rules, Cursor Docs https://cursor.com/docs/rules Model Context Protocol MCP , Cursor Docs https://cursor.com/docs/mcp Learn external sites Customizing agents, Cursor Learn https://cursor.com/learn/customizing agents"},{"title":"Create agents in Microsoft 365 Copilot","url":"setup-guides/create-agents-in-copilot/index.html","description":"Brief, Insight and Map run in Microsoft 365 Copilot, close to the SharePoint, Teams and Azure DevOps content they read.","text":"Who does it Laurence or Jon creates the three agents and shares them with the UX team. Marijana can do it as their delegate. What you need A Microsoft 365 Copilot licence. Agent Builder is part of Microsoft 365 Copilot; if you cannot see it, check your licence. The SharePoint site set up guide 4 /setup guides/sharepoint site/ Azure DevOps connected to Copilot guide 9 /setup guides/azure devops connectors/ The agent definitions in /agents : brief.md , insight.md and map.md Two ways to build Agent Builder , inside the Microsoft 365 Copilot app. No code: describe the agent in plain language, or configure it by hand. This is the place to start. Copilot Studio , for more control: tools, triggers and connections to other systems. Use it if an agent should start by itself when a status changes, rather than when a designer asks. The proposal is to build Brief, Insight and Map in Agent Builder first, and move them to Copilot Studio only if they need triggers. Steps 1. Open the Microsoft 365 Copilot app and choose Create agent it may show as New agent under Agents . 2. Choose to configure it yourself rather than describe it, so the agent matches its definition exactly. 3. Name and describe it : for example \"Brief\" and \"Turns a product feature into a UX problem and chooses the route.\" 4. Paste the instructions from the agent's definition in /agents : its job, steps, never and done when. Use the outline below. 5. Add knowledge : the SharePoint libraries it reads UX briefs, Discovery, Product packs and the Azure DevOps connection for Brief. 6. Add starter prompts , such as \"Write the UX brief for feature number \". 7. Test it in the preview on a test feature, and check it stops where its human gate says. 8. Create it and share it with the UX team: Laurence, Jon, Marijana, Katerina, Andrej and Mark. 9. Repeat for Insight and Map. The LogRocket step of Insight runs in Cursor guide 10 /setup guides/create agents in cursor/ . markdown \"Instructions outline for a Copilot agent\" You are the agent name agent in the OGI UX agentic design workflow. Job: one sentence from /agents/ agent .md When you are asked to start: triggered by Read: sources Steps: 1. step 2. step Never: never You are done when: done when Then stop. Human gate decides what happens next. Done when Brief, Insight and Map exist in Microsoft 365 Copilot, are shared with the UX team, and have each run once on a test feature. Docs external sites Build agents with Agent Builder in Microsoft 365 Copilot, Microsoft Learn https://learn.microsoft.com/en us/microsoft 365 copilot/extensibility/agent builder build agents Build your own agent with Microsoft 365 Copilot, Microsoft Support https://support.microsoft.com/en us/microsoft 365 copilot/build your own agent with microsoft 365 copilot Create and deploy an agent in Copilot Studio, Microsoft Learn https://learn.microsoft.com/en us/microsoft copilot studio/fundamentals get started Watch external sites Create your first agent in Copilot Studio, Mastering Copilot Studio on Microsoft Learn https://learn.microsoft.com/en us/shows/mastering copilot studio/create first agent copilot studio Agent demo videos, Microsoft Adoption https://adoption.microsoft.com/agents/success kit/"},{"title":"Run a feature end to end","url":"how-to-guides/run-a-feature-end-to-end/index.html","description":"From Ready for design to Handed over, on the full route.","text":"Before you start The feature needs its fields complete: problem, outcome, products, users, priority, quarter and constraints. Each product it touches needs a product pack. Discover 1. Product sets the feature to Ready for design. Brief checks the fields, finds the product packs, chooses the route and writes the UX brief. 2. The PM confirms scope. A designer moves the status to Discovery. 3. Insight synthesises research and pulls LogRocket baselines. Designers validate the insights. 4. Map maps the journeys and writes the screen inventory and design worklist. Designers approve, then move the status to Discovery approved. 5. Archive writes the discovery summary into the repo. Explore 1. Range builds 5 to 10 greybox directions in the sandbox and posts an index. 2. Critic stress tests each direction and recommends up to three. 3. Shortlist gate. Designers, the PM and an engineer agree direction, scope and early feasibility. Status: Shortlist agreed. Design 1. Designers make the anchor screens in Figma. The status moves to Anchors ready. 2. Extend builds out the remaining screens in batches of up to five. Designers review every batch. 3. Align checks each batch for consistency. Designers decide what to fix. 4. Design sign off gate. Designers, the PM and an engineer agree the design meets the success measures and is buildable. Status: Design signed off. Prototype and test 1. Build builds the prototype in the product's styling mode and posts the preview. 2. Audit runs axe, keyboard, contrast and Buckholt checks. A critical accessibility issue blocks testing. 3. Observe drafts the test plan. Designers run the sessions; Observe tags the recordings and writes findings. 4. Findings go back to Extend or Build until designers mark testing complete. Hand over 1. Relay writes the specs and UX acceptance criteria and adds them to each engineering story. 2. Relay sets Design status on the feature to Handed over. 3. After release, Relay checks the build against the design and the success measures, and posts the report."},{"title":"Fast-track a change","url":"how-to-guides/fast-track-a-change/index.html","description":"Go from Discover straight to Design when a change is small and well understood.","text":"When a change can be fast tracked Brief chooses the route and records why. A change can take the fast track only when all three are true: there is no new flow no new research is needed only existing screens change If any one is false, the feature takes the full route. What is skipped On the fast track, the feature goes from Discover straight to Design. Explore is skipped: Range runs only on full route items, so there are no sandbox explorations, no Critic review and no shortlist gate. What is not skipped The UX brief, discovery and the design worklist. The design sign off gate, with designers, the PM and an engineer. Prototype and handoff, including Audit's checks. Handoff by Relay. Anchor screens on the fast track The proposal: because only existing screens change, the current screens act as the anchors. Designers mark the frames being changed as anchors in Figma, update them where the change needs a new decision, and move the status to Anchors ready. Extend then works as usual."},{"title":"Add a non-Buckholt component","url":"how-to-guides/add-a-non-buckholt-component/index.html","description":"When Buckholt has no component for what the design needs, add it as a flagged, registered extension.","text":"Check Buckholt first Look in Buckholt core, its HTML and CSS reference and its documentation. Only build an extension when nothing there fits. Then check the extensions register: if an entry already exists, reuse it. In Figma Extend builds the component on the Extensions page with an x/ prefix and adds a register entry. Library components are always used as instances; they are never detached or changed. In code Build builds it in /extensions/ product / : 1. Use Buckholt tokens CSS custom properties only. No hard coded colours, spacing, radii or fonts. 2. Prefix every class with x . 3. Add data buckholt=\"false\" to the component's root element. 4. Add or complete the register entry, so it has both its Figma half and its code half. css \"Tokens only\" .x example { padding: var padding 05 ; border: var border width solid var ui border 02 ; border radius: var border radius md ; color: var text primary ; } Checks Align checks that extension components are flagged and register entries have both halves. Audit flags hard coded values and x components with no register entry. Relay maps each element to a Storybook component or a flagged extension at handoff."},{"title":"Onboard a legacy product","url":"how-to-guides/onboard-a-legacy-product/index.html","description":"Bring a product that is not on Buckholt into the workflow without changing how it looks.","text":"What a legacy product needs Captured legacy CSS in /legacy/ product . It is locked like Buckholt: no agent writes to it, and changing it needs the owner's approval. A product pack that records the product's styling mode: Legacy or Hybrid. Its legacy Figma library , connected so designers can make anchor screens and Extend and Align can use it. A migration map in /context , which Archive keeps and Build and Align read. How the agents treat it Brief stops if a product pack is missing. Build uses the legacy CSS in Legacy mode. In Hybrid mode it follows the pack and labels every Buckholt element. Align flags styling mode mismatches, such as Buckholt parts in a legacy screen. Audit checks markup against the legacy CSS instead of the Buckholt reference. Steps The proposed procedure: 1. Capture the legacy CSS into /legacy/ product , and add the folder to the locks so no agent can write to it. The product owner approves the capture. 2. Write the product pack in /context : the styling mode Legacy or Hybrid , the product's terms, its navigation pattern and its key screens. Laurence or Jon approves it, and Archive copies it to SharePoint. 3. Connect the legacy Figma library , so designers can make anchor screens and Extend and Align can use it. 4. Start the migration map : each legacy component and its Buckholt equivalent, or \"none yet\". 5. Run one feature as a pilot. Use Align's styling mode checks and Audit's checks against the legacy CSS to find gaps in the pack and the map. 6. Fix the gaps , then open the workflow to every feature for that product."},{"title":"Update a product pack","url":"how-to-guides/update-a-product-pack/index.html","description":"Change a product pack in the repo, get it approved, and Archive updates the SharePoint copy.","text":"Steps 1. Change the pack in /context on a branch and open a pull request. 2. Laurence or Jon reviews and approves it. Every product pack change needs that approval. 3. Merge the pull request. 4. Archive updates the pack's SharePoint copy so the two match. What Archive never does Change a pack without approval. Delete history. Keeping packs current In its weekly check, Archive flags packs older than 90 days. Every agent reads the product pack, so an out of date pack affects every feature for that product."}];
